{
  "account": "plur",
  "background": {"color": "#0A0A0A"},
  "slides": [
    {
      "type": "cover",
      "main": "They Made Music a Crime.",
      "sub": "UK Criminal Justice Act, 1994"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "text": [
        "May 1992.",
        "A field in England.",
        "20,000 people.",
        "No one sent invitations."
      ],
      "sub": "The sound was playing. That was enough."
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "text": [
        "Castlemorton Common.",
        "One week. It did not end.",
        "Spiral Tribe. Bedlam. DiY Sound System."
      ],
      "sub": "The government panicked."
    },
    {
      "type": "quote",
      "quote": [
        "Music characterised by the emission",
        "of a succession of repetitive beats."
      ],
      "note": "The legal definition. Techno became illegal."
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "text": [
        "Police could stop anyone within 8 kilometres.",
        "Preparing for a rave was enough to be arrested.",
        "20 people. A repetitive beat.",
        "That was a crime."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "text": [
        "Autechre wrote 65 beat patterns into one track.",
        "Proof it was not repetitive.",
        "The sleeve note read:",
        "bring a lawyer and a musicologist."
      ],
      "sub": "They fought back with sound."
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "text": [
        "The Prodigy named it.",
        "Their Law.",
        "The law could not stop the music.",
        "It only drove it underground."
      ],
      "sub": "And the underground built everything."
    },
    {
      "type": "cta",
      "text": [
        "Free parties still happen.",
        "The law never caught up."
      ],
      "strobe": "Music outlasted the act.",
      "cta": "Save this. You will want to come back."
    }
  ]
}
